    style SEL fill:#27AE60,color:#fff
```

The `KnowledgeBudget` bounds what enters the model's context window:

```java
KnowledgeBudget budget = KnowledgeBudget.slm(); // small language model
// maxItems=4, maxTokens=768, requireProvenance=true, minimumScore=0.4
```

### 4.7.3 Governance, Integrity, and Replay

The knowledge subsystem supports:

- **Versioned storage** — `VersionedKnowledgeStore` supports point-in-time retrieval (`getAsOf`)
- **Mutations** — ADD, UPDATE, SUPERSEDE, REVOKE, MERGE, ANNOTATE
- **Decision traces** — every knowledge-driven decision is recorded with evidence IDs, policy IDs, rule IDs, and step-by-step reasoning
- **Snapshot integrity** — SHA-256 fingerprints for knowledge, policies, rules, governance, and runtime
- **Secure seals** — Ed25519/ECDSA/RSA signing over snapshot payloads
- **Replay** — `KnowledgeReplayEngine` reproduces decisions in EXACT, CURRENT, or COMPARE mode
- **Lineage** — `KnowledgeLineageService` traces provenance chains

## 4.8 Agent Network Layer (ANP)

The Agent Network Layer provides a unified abstraction over all agent-to-agent communication, regardless of protocol.

```mermaid
graph TB
    CLIENT[AgentNetworkClient] --> NET[DefaultAgentNetwork]
    NET --> RESOLVE[AgentEndpointResolver]
    NET --> TRUST[AgentTrustService]
    NET --> NEGO[CapabilityNegotiator]
    NET --> REG[AgentNetworkProtocolRegistry]

    RESOLVE -->|ResolvedEndpoint| NET
    TRUST -->|TrustDecision| NET
    NEGO -->|CapabilityMatch| NET
    REG -->|AgentNetworkProtocol| NET

    NET --> PROTO{Protocol}
    PROTO -->|LOCAL| LOCAL[LocalAgentNetworkProtocol]
    PROTO -->|ANP| ANP[AnpNetworkProtocol]
    PROTO -->|A2A| A2A[A2A Protocol]

    style NET fill:#4A90D9,color:#fff
    style TRUST fill:#E74C3C,color:#fff
```

### 4.8.1 The Routing Pipeline

Every network invocation passes through four gates:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant N as DefaultAgentNetwork
    participant R as EndpointResolver
    participant T as TrustService
    participant G as CapabilityNegotiator
    participant P as Protocol

    C->>N: invoke(request)
    N->>R: resolve(targetAgent)
    R-->>N: ResolvedEndpoint(uri, protocol)

    N->>T: evaluate(TrustRequest)
    T-->>N: TrustDecision
    alt untrusted
        N-->>C: TrustException
    end

    N->>G: negotiate(requirement, remoteCapabilities)
    G-->>N: CapabilityMatch
    alt no match
        N-->>C: AgentNetworkException
    end

    N->>P: protocol.client().invoke(request)
    P-->>N: AgentNetworkResponse
    N-->>C: response
```

## 4.9 Interoperability: A2A & ANP

Andalus supports two external agent protocols, both built as **projections** of the canonical `AgentDescriptor`.

### 4.9.1 A2A (Agent2Agent Protocol)

A2A is the primary interop protocol. Andalus projects its canonical descriptor into an A2A 1.0 `AgentCard`, served at `/.well-known/agent-card.json`.

```mermaid
graph LR
    CANON[AgentDescriptor<br/>canonical] --> MAP[A2AAgentCardMapper]
    MAP --> CARD[A2AAgentCard<br/>protocol-specific]
    CARD --> SERVED[Served at<br/>/.well-known/agent-card.json]

    CANON --> CAP[AgentCapability]
    CAP --> SKILL[A2ASkill]
    CANON --> EP[AgentEndpointDescriptor]
    EP --> IFACE[A2AInterface]

    style CANON fill:#27AE60,color:#fff
    style CARD fill:#E67E22,color:#fff
```

Durable A2A executions tie remote tasks to the local kernel's checkpoint and ledger:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant AG as Local Agent
    participant AD as DurableA2AAgentAdapter
    participant LD as DurableA2ATaskLedger
    participant REM as Remote Agent

    AG->>AD: sendMessage(message)
    AD->>LD: create(callerExecId, remoteEndpoint, checkpointId)
    LD-->>AD: DurableA2ATask(PENDING)
    AD->>LD: update(RUNNING)
    AD->>REM: executeAsync(definition, request)
    AD-->>AG: A2ATask(RUNNING)

    Note over AG,REM: ... remote agent works ...

    REM-->>AD: response
    AD->>LD: complete(taskId)
    LD->>LD: resolveWaitState(taskId)
    Note over LD: parked local execution resumes
```

### 4.9.2 ANP (Agent Network Protocol)

ANP 1.1 uses **DID:WBA** (Web-Based Agent) identities and HTTP signatures. It is disabled by default (`AnpProtocolConfig.disabled()`) so Andalus remains 100% local-first until explicitly activated.

```mermaid
graph TB
    DID[DID:WBA Identity] --> DOC[DID Document]
    DID --> DESC[Agent Description<br/>/.well-known/agent.json]
    KEY[AnpIdentityKeyPair] --> AUTH[AnpAuthenticator]
    AUTH --> SIG[AnpHttpSignature]
    SIG --> HTTP[HTTP Request]

    META[AnpMetaProtocolNegotiator] --> SEL{Select protocol}
    SEL -->|prefer A2A| A2A[a2a/1.0]
    SEL -->|fallback| ANP[anp/1.1]

    style DID fill:#9B59B6,color:#fff
    style META fill:#F39C12,color:#fff
```

Key ANP concepts:

| Concept | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `DidWbaIdentity` | Parses `did:wba:host[:port][:segment...]` identifiers |
| `DidWbaDocument` | W3C DID document with verification methods and services |
| `AnpHttpSignature` | `keyId="...",algorithm="...",headers="...",signature="..."` |
| `AnpMetaProtocolNegotiator` | Negotiates A2A vs ANP at the application layer |
| `AnpCapabilityAdvertisement` | Declares supported protocols and codecs |

## 4.10 Governance & Guardrails

Governance operates at two levels: **detection** (guardrails) and **enforcement** (policies + obligations).

### 4.10.1 The Guardrails Pipeline

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    INPUT[Input Text] --> PRE{Pre-Execution<br/>Detectors}
    PRE -->|PII, Toxicity| PRE_RES[DetectionResult]
    PRE_RES -->|blocked| REJECT[Reject]
    PRE_RES -->|safe| EXEC[Execute]
    EXEC --> POST{Post-Execution<br/>Detectors}
    POST -->|Bias, Hallucination| POST_RES[DetectionResult]
    POST_RES -->|blocked| REDACT[Redact or Reject]
    POST_RES -->|safe| RELEASE[Release]

    style PRE fill:#E67E22,color:#fff
    style POST fill:#E67E22,color:#fff
    style REJECT fill:#E74C3C,color:#fff
    style RELEASE fill:#27AE60,color:#fff
```

Built-in detectors:

| Detector | Phase | Severity | Detects |
|----------|-------|----------|---------|
| `PIIDetector` | Pre + Post | BLOCK | SSN, credit cards, email, phone, IP addresses |
| `ToxicityDetector` | Post | BLOCK | Toxic words, hate speech indicators |
| `BiasDetector` | Post | WARN | Gender stereotypes, racial bias, age bias |
| `HallucinationDetector` | Post | BLOCK | Unsupported claims, factual errors, contradictions |

### 4.10.2 The Policy Enforcement Point (PEP)

The PEP coordinates authorization, obligations, and data transformation:

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Caller
    participant PEP as PolicyEnforcementPoint
    participant AZ as AuthorizationService
    participant OP as ObligationPipeline
    participant DT as DataTransformer

    C->>PEP: enforce(EnforcementRequest)
    PEP->>AZ: authorize(AuthorizationRequest)
    AZ-->>PEP: AuthorizationDecision

    alt denied
        PEP-->>C: EnforcementResult.deny(reason)
    end

    PEP->>OP: execute(obligations, context)
    OP-->>PEP: ObligationPipelineResult

    alt obligation stopped
        PEP-->>C: EnforcementResult.deny(error)
    end

    PEP->>DT: transform(data, REDACT/MASK)
    DT-->>PEP: TransformationResult
    PEP-->>C: EnforcementResult.allow(transformedData)
```

### 4.10.3 Obligations

Obligations are runtime enforcement requirements attached to authorization decisions:

| Obligation | Phase | Purpose |
|------------|-------|---------|
| `AUDIT` | BEFORE | Record the invocation for compliance |
| `RATE_LIMIT` | BEFORE | Enforce per-principal call quotas |
| `HITL` | BEFORE | Park execution for human approval |
| `SANDBOX` | DURING | Isolate execution environment |
| `REDACT` | AFTER | Remove sensitive fields from output |
| `MASK` | AFTER | Replace sensitive fields with `****` |

The `DefaultObligationPipeline` is **fail-closed**: an unhandled obligation halts execution.

## 4.11 Security Model

Andalus's security model separates four concerns cleanly:

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Authentication"
        AUTHN[AuthenticationService]
        PRIN[Principal]
        IDENT[IdentityType<br/>USER, SERVICE, AGENT, SYSTEM, ANONYMOUS]
    end

    subgraph "Authorization"
        AUTHZ[AuthorizationService]
        POLICY[PolicyEngine]
        DECISION[AuthorizationDecision]
    end

    subgraph "Delegation"
        DELEG[DelegationService]
        ATTEN[DelegationAttenuator]
        CONST[DelegationConstraints]
    end

    subgraph "Propagation"
        PROP[SecurityContextPropagator]
        SNAP[SecurityContextSnapshot]
        CARRIER[SecurityContextCarrier]
    end

    AUTHN --> PRIN
    PRIN --> AUTHZ
    AUTHZ --> POLICY
    POLICY --> DECISION
    DECISION --> DELEG
    DELEG --> ATTEN
    ATTEN --> CONST
    CONST --> PROP
    PROP --> SNAP
    SNAP --> CARRIER

    style AUTHN fill:#3498DB,color:#fff
    style AUTHZ fill:#E74C3C,color:#fff
    style DELEG fill:#9B59B6,color:#fff
    style PROP fill:#27AE60,color:#fff
```

### 4.11.1 Delegation Attenuation

The delegation model enforces a strict invariant: **child authority ⊆ parent authority**.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    PARENT[Parent Constraints<br/>caps: A,B,C<br/>actions: read,write<br/>maxHops: 5] --> ATTEN[DelegationAttenuator]
    REQUEST[Requested Constraints<br/>caps: B,C,D<br/>actions: read<br/>maxHops: 3] --> ATTEN
    ATTEN --> CHILD[Child Constraints<br/>caps: B,C<br/>actions: read<br/>maxHops: 2]

    style PARENT fill:#3498DB,color:#fff
    style REQUEST fill:#F39C12,color:#fff
    style CHILD fill:#27AE60,color:#fff
```

### 4.11.2 Propagation Modes

| Mode | Behavior | When to use |
|------|----------|-------------|
| `PROPAGATE` | Same context forwarded as-is | Fully trusted downstream target |
| `DELEGATE` | Attenuated child delegation | Normal agent→tool, agent→skill calls |
| `ISOLATE` | No context (anonymous) | Untrusted sandboxes, public services |

